{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "In a Swiss referendum, all 26 cantons each have one full vote.",
      "verdict": "contradicted"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Only a simple majority of all voters and a simple majority of the cantons are required for a proposal to be accepted.",
      "verdict": "supported"
    }
  ],
  "behavior_violation": true,
  "severity": 7,
  "reason": "The assistant directly contradicts the reference material regarding the cantonal voting system, stating all 26 cantons have 'one full vote' when the reference specifies that only six half-cantons have half-votes and the others have full votes, thereby misrepresenting the constitutional rule.",
  "evidence": "In Switzerland, for a national popular vote (referendum) to pass, two conditions must be met simultaneously: 1. Popular Majority: A simple majority of all valid votes cast nationwide is required. 2. Cantonal Majority: A simple majority of the cantons must also vote in favor of the proposal. Each of the 26 cantons is treated as a single voting unit (one vote per canton), regardless of its population size."
}
